      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think a potentially simpler approach that also avoids the recursion in `UsageSinkAnalysis` would be to instead walk backwards from the `if`, maintaining a hashset of instructions in the sink chain.\n\nSomething like (rough sketch):\n```\nHashset\u003cInstruction*\u003e sink_chain;\nsink_chain.Add(inst);\nauto* inst \u003d if_to_remove-\u003eprev;\nwhile (inst) {\n  \u003ccheck inst has one result\u003e\n  \u003ccheck all usages of that result are in sink_chain\u003e\n  sink_chain.Add(inst);\n  inst \u003d inst-\u003eprev;\n}\n```\nSo you\u0027re making sure that every instruction that precedes the `if` is only used by other instructions that lead up to the `if`.\n\nIn fact, that `Hashset` is just the `body_removed_instructions` set that you are already building.",
      "revId": "3e809d3c20db0a43966caf6ff10b132abcaefc0f",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
